          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is it ever correct to submit a code root without its decimal characters?",
          "answer": "Yes, but only for codes that are complete at three characters. The retrieved guidance says a code is three to seven characters long, and a decimal point follows the first three characters only when more characters follow. So I10 is written without a decimal, while E11.9 has one. (*ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*)\n\nI10 (essential, or primary, hypertension) is the example the sources give. It takes no fourth, fifth or sixth character, so it is a complete code as written. (*Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*)\n\nMost categories are different. Three-character categories such as E11 are not complete on their own. They need more characters to add detail such as cause, site or severity, so a bare category is not a full code. (*ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*; *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*)\n\nThe retrieved passages do not cover how claims are submitted or how any particular billing system handles decimals. For that, check the rules of the payer or billing system, or ask a certified medical coder.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
